# Section 3: Structure of the Related Tables in the Database

## 3.1 Database Overview

Our Final Grade Calculator will use MySQL as its relational database management system.

A relational database organizes information into tables containing rows and columns.

Our proposed database will store course information and assignment grades.

The database will use primary keys to uniquely identify records and foreign keys to establish relationships between related tables.

The database will be shared by the application's backend components.

## 3.2 Courses Table

**Assigned Team Member:** Malachi Johnson

**Related Component:** Course Management Component

**Table Name:** Courses

The Courses table will store information about the courses entered into the Final Grade Calculator.

| Column Name | Data Type | Key | Description |
|---|---|---|---|
| course_id | INT | Primary Key | Unique identifier for each course. |
| course_name | VARCHAR(100) | | Name of the course. |
| course_code | VARCHAR(20) | | Course identification code. |
| credit_hours | INT | | Number of course credit hours. |

**Purpose:**

The Courses table will allow the application to organize and maintain course information.

Each course will receive a unique course ID that can be referenced by the Assignments table.

## 3.3 Assignments Table

**Assigned Team Member:** Jalen Davis

**Related Component:** Grade Calculation Component

**Table Name:** Assignments

The Assignments table will store assignment information and the grades associated with each assignment.

| Column Name | Data Type | Key | Description |
|---|---|---|---|
| assignment_id | INT | Primary Key | Unique identifier for each assignment. |
| course_id | INT | Foreign Key | References the associated course. |
| assignment_name | VARCHAR(100) | | Name of the assignment. |
| category | VARCHAR(50) | | Assignment category. |
| weight | DECIMAL(5,2) | | Percentage weight used in grade calculations. |
| score | DECIMAL(5,2) | | Points earned by the student. |
| max_score | DECIMAL(5,2) | | Maximum possible points. |

**Purpose:**

The Assignments table will store assignment grades and connect each assignment to its associated course.

The Grade Calculation Component will retrieve this information to calculate current grades and estimate final course grades.

## 3.4 Database Relationships

The Courses and Assignments tables will have a one-to-many relationship.

One course can contain multiple assignments, but each assignment will belong to one course.

The `course_id` column in the Assignments table will act as a foreign key referencing the `course_id` primary key in the Courses table.

**Relationship:**

```text
COURSES
-------
course_id (PK)
course_name
course_code
credit_hours
       |
       | One-to-Many
       |
       v
ASSIGNMENTS
-----------
assignment_id (PK)
course_id (FK)
assignment_name
category
weight
score
max_score
```

## 3.5 How the Database Supports Grade Calculations

The database will provide the information needed to calculate student grades.

For example, when a student enters assignment scores, the application will retrieve the assignment information and its associated grading weight.

The Grade Calculation Component will use this information to calculate the current weighted grade.

The application will also use the available grading information to estimate final grades and determine the score required on a final examination.

For weighted grade calculations, the application will use the assignment scores and applicable grading weights. Missing assignments will be handled according to the grading rules configured for the course.

## 3.6 Database Summary

The shared MySQL database will provide centralized storage for the Final Grade Calculator.

The Courses table will organize course information, while the Assignments table will store assignment details and associated grades.

The relationship between these tables will allow the application to retrieve all assignments associated with a specific course.

The database will support the Grade Calculation and Course Management components by providing the information necessary for application operations.

